WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:05.000
Lesson 0.2 · Start Here: Your Founder Journey
Your founding profile

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.800
One page of plain facts about what you are building, which people read first.

3
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:28.133
A founding profile is one page of plain facts about what you are building. It is not a pitch: it is the page people read first to decide whether, and how, they can work with you.

4
00:00:28.533 --> 00:00:29.133
What goes on the page

5
00:00:29.133 --> 00:00:41.574
Name and sentence: Your registered or working name, and what you will do, for whom and where. For example: “We will run after-school science clubs for girls aged 10 to 14 in two districts.”

6
00:00:41.574 --> 00:00:53.644
Form and stage: The legal form you intend, how far along you are and who is involved so far. For example: A cooperative, not yet registered; testing stage; three co-founders and two volunteers.

7
00:00:53.644 --> 00:01:06.456
Activities and places: What you plan to do, with whom and where. Flag children, personal data or public money. For example: Weekly tutoring for teenagers in a library room; we will hold parents’ contact details.

8
00:01:06.456 --> 00:01:17.133
Still to decide: Open decisions, written as questions rather than guesses. For example: Should we register as an association or stay informal for the first year?

9
00:01:17.533 --> 00:01:18.133
Why it matters

10
00:01:18.133 --> 00:01:23.807
1. You will give these facts in applications, forms and first conversations.

11
00:01:23.807 --> 00:01:29.481
2. Your legal form shapes control, surplus, fundraising and who regulates you.

12
00:01:29.481 --> 00:01:35.415
3. Your stage decides how much formality you need now.

13
00:01:35.815 --> 00:01:36.415
Where you will reuse it

14
00:01:36.415 --> 00:01:40.607
1. Funding applications, bank accounts and registration forms.

15
00:01:40.607 --> 00:01:44.800
2. The About page of your first website.

16
00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:51.474
3. Your choice of legal form and, later, registration and legal duties.

17
00:01:51.874 --> 00:01:52.474
What to avoid

18
00:01:52.474 --> 00:01:58.148
Writing a pitch or a mission statement instead of plain facts.

19
00:01:58.148 --> 00:02:02.341
Guessing answers you have not decided yet.

20
00:02:02.341 --> 00:02:09.385
Registering only because a template, a friend or a funder suggests it.

21
00:02:09.985 --> 00:02:11.785
You write your founding profile in six short parts.

22
00:02:11.785 --> 00:02:15.785
Then save it, and download it as a PDF.

23
00:02:17.285 --> 00:02:20.385
Method into Impact
Better foundations. Stronger organisations.
